The stability boundary of delayed proportional correction. Panel (a) plots the critical gain $g^\star(k)=2\sin[\pi/(2(2k+1))]$ against the information lag $k$; the dashed curve is the large-$k$ asymptote $\pi/(2k+1)$ and the open marker is $g^\star(0)=2$, the no-delay case. Panel (b) shows the spectral radius of $z^{k+1}-z^{k}+g$ over the $(g,k)$ plane, with the analytic boundary drawn over it: the change of colour across the heavy line is the crossing established in [[REF:prop:delay]]. The boundary falls roughly as $1/k$, so a coordinator who doubles the age of his information halves the maximum gain he can run. Here $k$ is the effective delay of the loop, related to the simulator's reporting lag by $k=\ell+1$; the point $k=0$ is the theoretical no-delay benchmark and is not attainable by any mechanism.
